50\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE CHECK\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSTEP 1.5-1. Base Rates (reference rates per framework; [Estimate] where updated)\n- Cyclical peak \u2192 40%+ share-price drawdown within 3 years: ~30\u201340% [Reference]\n- Mid-cycle/peak operating margin persistence among industrial cyclicals for 5+ years: ~25\u201330% [Reference]\n- Aerospace supplier surviving 5 years at near-record margins without a down-cycle test:\n  ~50\u201360% [Estimate]\n- Platform-transition success: N/A (ATRO is not a platform)\n- Large M&A ROIC > WACC: N/A (BMA is a small tuck-in; no $10B+ deal)\n\nSTEP 1.5-2. Deviation Check\n- My base case assumes OI margin 15.3% (2026) \u2192 17.0% (2030), i.e., sustained margins ~9\u201310x\n  the 10-year median (1.8%). This DEVIATES from the base rate that margins mean-revert.\n  Justifications (3+):\n  1. The company exited non-core contract manufacturing, low-margin mass-transit work, and aerospace\n     simplification programs (Q2 2026 release: prior-year had $5.8M simplification charge and $6.9M\n     transit-contract hit [Actual]).\n  2. Product mix shifted toward IFEC/seat-motion/power content per aircraft, which has historically\n     higher margins (Q2 2026 Aerospace adj. operating margin 21.4% [Actual]).\n  3. The 0% convertible refinancings removed material interest drag vs 2020\u20132023 (Q2 2026 interest\n     expense down 24.7% YoY [Actual]; 2031 converts 0% coupon [Actual \u2014 RTTNews/company 10-Q]).\n- Even WITH this favorable deviation (i.e., margins stay at 17%), IV \u2248 $42 \u2014 below T0.\n- If margins instead revert toward the 10-year median (bear), IV \u2248 $19.\n\nSTEP 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check\n- Probability embedded in T0 that ATRO sustains peak-ish margins through 2030: >60% implied.\n  Base rate: ~25\u201330%. Probability > 2.0x base rate \u2192 RED FLAG: requiring further evidence of\n  structural margin permanence. No such evidence exists beyond two good years (2025\u20132026H1).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSTEP 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences)\n- ATRO designs/manufactures electrical power, lighting/IFEC, seat-motion, avionics and rugged\n  computing products for commercial aircraft, business jets, military platforms, and test systems.\n- It sells highly-engineered, safety-certified components; revenues are tied to aircraft production\n  rates and per-aircraft content, plus defense/test budgets.\n- Understandability: HIGH (physical products, clear end-markets, certification barriers visible).\n\nSTEP 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics (0\u20133 each)\n- Intangible assets (IP, certifications):       2\n- Switching costs (design-in, FAA/DO-178):     2\n- Network effects:                              0\n- Cost advantage:                               1\n- Economies of scale:                           1\n- Regulatory barriers:                          2\n- Data moat:                                    1\n- MOAT SCORE: 9/21 \u2192 NARROW moat\n- Moat direction: WIDENING \u2014 evidenced by record backlog $780.6M [Actual], Q2 book-to-bill 1.18\n  [Actual], 4 consecutive record-backlog quarters, higher content per aircraft (IFEC, seat motion,\n  power), BMA motor acquisition adding technology breadth.\n  Narrowing threats: larger players (TransDigm, HEICO, Safran, Collins) could attack IFEC/power\n  niches; concentration of success in Commercial Transport (78% of Aerospace sales).\n\nSTEP 2-3. Lynch Classification\n- CYCLICAL (emerging from Turnaround) \u2014 2020\u20132023 four consecutive loss years; 2024\u20132026 recovery.\n- Checkpoints: (1) cycle position: mid-to-late up-cycle in commercial deliveries\n  (Boeing 737 47/mo \u2192 63 target; Airbus \u2192 75 [Estimate]); (2) don't pay peak multiple on peak\n  earnings \u2014 the cardinal rule for cyclicals; (3) monitor book-to-bill and margin spread vs\n  prior cycle peaks.\n\nSTEP 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n- Revenue growth: 3-yr CAGR (FY22\u2013FY25) 17.2% [Arithmetic]; FY25 +8.4% [Actual]; Q2-26 +27.0% [Actual] \u2014 EXCELLENT\n- Gross margin: FY25 29.9% [Actual]; Q2-26 33.4% [Actual]; 760bp YoY expansion \u2014 EXCELLENT (current)\n- Operating margin: FY25 8.8% [Actual]; Q2-26 15.6% GAAP / 16.6% adj [Actual]; 10-yr median 1.8% [Actual] \u2014 GOOD (current), CONCERNING (long-run)\n- EPS growth: FY25 diluted EPS \u2248 $0.82 [Arithmetic; 29/35.4]; H1-26 diluted EPS \u2248 $1.68 [Actual 60.6/36.1] \u2014 EXCELLENT (recovery-driven)\n- ROE: FY25 GAAP 14.6% (NI 29 / avg equity 198) [Arithmetic]; vendor ROE 33.7% (trailing) [Estimate] \u2014 GOOD, but equity base tiny ($140M FY25 [Actual])\n- ROIC (GAAP): FY25 NOPAT \u2248 34 / avg invested capital \u2248 482 \u2192 7.1% [Estimate] \u2014 ADEQUATE, improving\n- FCF margin / conversion: FY25 OCF 75 \u2212 capex 32 = 43 [Actual]; FCF after SBC 36 [Arithmetic]; OCF/NI = 259% [Arithmetic] \u2014 GOOD on OCF; WEAK if WC growth continues\n- Net debt/EBITDA: net debt \u2248360 / 2026E EBITDA \u2248195 \u2192 1.85x [Estimate] \u2014 MANAGEABLE\n- Net debt vs cash: NET DEBT [Actual cash 18; LT debt 334 + op leases 44; vendor total debt 378]\n- Interest coverage: \u224813x on 2026E [Estimate] \u2014 GOOD\n- Overall: GOOD (recovering), with balance-sheet and durability caveats.\n\nSTEP 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0\u20133 each, /15)\n1. Buyback efficiency: 0 \u2014 net issuer: +4.1% shares 1-yr; +3.1% 3-yr CAGR [Actual]\n2. M&A track record: 1 \u2014 BMA acquisition (Oct-2025) and prior divestitures; limited multi-cycle evidence\n3. Capex efficiency: 1 \u2014 capex rising from $32M (FY25) to \u2248$55M (2026E); incremental returns unproven\n4. Mix rationality: 2 \u2014 financing growth with 0% converts at scale is shareholder-friendly [Actual]\n5. Guidance accuracy: 2 \u2014 recent beats/raises (Q1/Q2 2026, guidance raised to $1.02\u20131.04B [Actual])\n   vs 2020\u20132023 repeated misses with withdrawals\nTOTAL: 6/15 \u2014 ADEQUATE\nInsider bonus: CEO/management >5%? NO (insider 1.92% [Actual]) | 6-mo net buying? [Unconfirmed]\nRecent large insider sell? [Unconfirmed]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n2.5-1 R&D capitalization: R&D is not broken out in our extract beyond the Q2 release (\"R&D was\n  down $0.7M\" [Actual]). R&D is expensed within SG&A (~$136\u2013140M/yr). Adjustment: capitalize 3-yr\n  R&D at \u2248$55\u201365M/year [Assumption]. This would raise adjusted OI by ~$20\u201325M and adjusted\n  invested capital by ~$120\u2013150M. Adjusted ROIC improves to \u224810\u201312% [Assumption].\n2.5-2 SBC treatment: FY25 SBC $7M [Actual], FY24 $9M [Actual], FY26E \u2248$10M [Assumption]. I\n  deduct SBC from FCF in all forecasts (see Section 3). Pack's owner-earnings warning is respected.\n2.5-3 Operating lease capitalization: FY25 operating leases $44M [Actual], up from $25M [Actual]\n  (BMA leases). I INCLUDE the $44M in net debt. Adjusted net debt \u2248 $360M [Estimate].\n2.5-4 Brand/data assets: not material for an engineered-components supplier; no adjustment [Assumption]\n2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC: \u224810\u201312% vs GAAP \u22487% [Assumption]; gap < 5pp, so no major intangibles issue.\n2.5-6 Owner Earnings (Buffett):\n  FY25: NI 29 + D&A 22 \u2212 maintenance capex \u224820 \u2212 \u0394WC 26 \u2212 SBC 7 \u2248 \u2212$2M [Estimate]\n  2026E: NI 121 + D&A 38 \u2212 maintenance capex \u224835 \u2212 \u0394WC 15 \u2212 SBC 10 \u2248 $99M [Estimate]\n  \u2192 The growth phase is consuming cash; owner earnings are only now inflecting positive.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 Engine 2 \u00d7 60% + Engine 1 \u00d7 40%\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nENGINE SELECTION REASONS (per Layer 0):\n- Archetype C because: (1) 10-yr OI margin range \u221220% to +16%; (2) 2020\u20132023 four consecutive\n  losses; (3) revenue fell 42% peak-to-trough (2018: $803M \u2192 2021: $445M); (4) ROIC history weak;\n  (5) classification as a quality compounder requires 3+ consecutive years of ROIC > WACC \u2014 not met.\n- Transition status: Cyclical \u2192 Quality is <50% complete. Criteria unmet: (a) ROIC > WACC for 3\n  consecutive years (only 2025\u20132026E qualify at best), (b) margin volatility not yet reduced\n  (one-year data only). Therefore blended engine per framework rules.\n- Errors if only Engine 1 (DCF) were used: DCF extrapolates current record margins; at 17% terminal\n  OI margins the DCF still yields IV $42 \u2014 but a DCF-only analyst would have missed the cyclical\n  mean-reversion risk. Errors if only Engine 2: would undervalue genuine structural improvements\n  (mix, refinancing, content growth).\n\n\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u2501\u